# Files whose line endings are load-bearing, and must survive a Windows checkout.
#
# git's core.autocrlf=true -- the default of the Git for Windows installer -- rewrites
# checked-out text files to CRLF. For a patch file that is fatal but silent: `git apply`
# compares context lines byte for byte against the LF-terminated engine sources, finds no
# match, and reports only "patch does not apply", with nothing to suggest that the patch
# itself was altered on the way to disk. Measured on a fresh clone: 52 CRLF, 0 LF.
*.patch text eol=lf
*.diff text eol=lf

# Shell scripts fail with a less mysterious but equally annoying error under CRLF
# ("/bin/bash^M: bad interpreter") when the repository is shared with WSL2.
*.sh text eol=lf
